WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.234
Browser cache and downloads connect web activity to files and local content.

2
00:00:05.234 --> 00:00:15.782
Cache may contain stored copies or fragments of web resources such as images, scripts, pages, style sheets, thumbnails, media segments, or other content.

3
00:00:15.782 --> 00:00:25.606
Downloads can show file names, source URLs, destination paths, timestamps, partial downloads, and sometimes status or interruption details.

4
00:00:25.606 --> 00:00:28.665
Cache evidence can be useful but misleading.

5
00:00:28.665 --> 00:00:35.188
A cached image may have loaded because it was embedded on a page, not because the user selected it.

6
00:00:35.188 --> 00:00:41.549
A script may be cached because an application required it, not because the user understood it.

7
00:00:41.549 --> 00:00:45.977
A cached page may be partial, stale, or automatically refreshed.

8
00:00:45.977 --> 00:00:50.809
Cache shows browser behavior, not necessarily deliberate attention.

9
00:00:50.809 --> 00:00:57.653
Download history often provides stronger evidence of user interaction, but it still needs context.

10
00:00:57.653 --> 00:01:07.638
A download record may include source URL, target path, start time, end time, file size, danger status, or interruption state depending on browser.

11
00:01:07.638 --> 00:01:11.664
Partial downloads may show attempts that did not complete.

12
00:01:11.664 --> 00:01:20.118
A file path can help connect the browser event to file system artifacts, but it does not prove the file was opened or used later.

13
00:01:20.118 --> 00:01:25.433
Cloud drive and webmail downloads add additional interpretation questions.

14
00:01:25.433 --> 00:01:35.095
A file may have come from a SaaS application, corporate storage, personal cloud account, email attachment, shared link, or automated export.

15
00:01:35.095 --> 00:01:46.207
The browser may show a downloaded file while the web application logs show who requested it, what account was used, and whether the action was expected under policy.

16
00:01:46.207 --> 00:01:49.186
File names and metadata should be verified.

17
00:01:49.186 --> 00:01:57.802
Extensions may be misleading, downloaded names can be changed, and browser-safe-name behavior may alter destination names.

18
00:01:57.802 --> 00:02:08.592
The strongest analysis correlates download records with file system metadata, hash values, endpoint logs, DLP records, application logs, and user context.
